yoyo now loads a subdirectory's instruction file (YOYO.md, CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and the rest of PROJECT_CONTEXT_FILES) the first time the model successfully reads a file under that directory. Build, the full cargo test, clippy with -D warnings and fmt --check all passed. The last full test run was before the second commit, which only adds one test, and that module's 10 tests pass. Two commits: ff4430ab (the feature) and 7edcf440 (one more test).

What changed
• New module, `src/nested_instructions.rs`. On a successful read_file, it checks every directory between the project root and the file, outermost first, but never the root itself (startup already loaded that). It appends any instruction files it finds as a new text block after the file's own content, with the same provenance markers the root files get.
  • Each directory is checked once per session, whether or not it had a file.
  • Each file is capped at 16,000 bytes, cut on a character boundary, and the text says how much was cut.
  • The root is the current directory at the moment of the read, so after /cd it moves.
• Same rules as a normal read. An instruction file that a read_file of its own path would refuse is skipped without a message: deny lists (from startup or /cd), FIFOs and devices, and the outside-launch-directory fence.
  • For that fence I added outside_read::check_without_asking, which uses the same decision and approvals as the normal check but never prompts. Asking the user to approve a file nobody requested would be a surprise prompt. This touches a file the task didn't list.
• Wired into both `GuardedTool` and `ArcGuardedTool` (the sub-agent wrapper), right after partial_read_guard::record.
• Smaller edits: context::instruction_boundary became pub(crate), the module line went into main.rs, the tool_wrappers.rs size entry went from 5746 to 5762 with a dated note, and docs/src/features/context.md has a new "Subdirectory instruction files" section.

Tests: 10 in the new module. They cover:
• a file found once, with a second read in the same directory adding nothing
• the root's own file never added
• two levels, outer first
• a file outside the root
• no instruction files: the tool result is unchanged (checked with assert_eq!)
• the cap falling in the middle of a 3-byte character
• the same read under two different roots giving different results
• other tools left alone
• the file-reading step against a real temp directory, including a deny-dir refusal

Check that the tests can fail: I made the note function always return nothing and ran the tests in one command. Exactly the 5 tests that expect a file to be found failed, and the 4 that expect nothing stayed green. After restoring it, all passed and no sabotage marker is left.

Not tested: the step that takes the current directory as the root inside append_for_read. It reads process-wide state, so it's checked only by the compiler and by reading the code. I haven't run it against a live model.

Out of scope (stated in the commit): applying these files on edit, giving each sub-agent its own record (it's shared across the process, so a sub-agent's read marks a directory as seen for the parent too), and /context listing these files.
